  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Tested Gutter Guards", link: "/best-gutter-guards/" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6"><TrustBadge /></div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">Autumn Job</span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">Field Tested</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">How to Clean Gutters <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Before Winter</span></h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          A gutter full of wet leaves in December is a fifty-kilo block of ice hanging off your fascia, and a downspout pouring water at your foundation for four months.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#timing" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">Start Here</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">Jump to the FAQ</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto"><SocialProof users="11.2k" /></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">In This Guide</h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#timing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. When to Do It</a>
          <a href="#why" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. What Clogged Gutters Actually Cost</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Ladder Safety First</a>
          <a href="#method" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. The Method</a>
          <a href="#downspouts" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Downspouts and Discharge</a>
          <a href="#inspect" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. The Inspection That Matters More</a>
          <a href="#ice-dams" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Ice Dams: The Real Cause</a>
          <a href="#guards" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Are Gutter Guards Worth It?</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Gutters exist to move a roof's worth of water away from a building's foundation. Blocked, they do the opposite with impressive efficiency — concentrating that water at the base of the wall, at exactly the time of year the ground is least able to take it.
        </p>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. When to Do It</h2>

          <p>
            After the leaves have finished falling and before the first hard freeze. In most temperate climates that means late autumn, and the window is narrower than people expect: too early and you will be doing it again in three weeks, too late and you are chipping frozen debris out of a gutter while standing on a ladder in the cold.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your house sits under heavy deciduous cover, plan on two visits — one mid-autumn when the first flush comes down, and one after the trees are bare. Pines are worse in their own way, because needles shed year-round and pack into a dense mat that water will not pass through.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pick a dry day a couple of days after rain. Wet debris is heavy and unpleasant to handle, but bone-dry debris blows everywhere. Slightly damp is the sweet spot, and a dry ladder footing matters more than either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. What Clogged Gutters Actually Cost</h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Failure</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Leads To</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Overflow at the eaves</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Water dumped at the foundation, basement damp</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Trapped water freezing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hangers pulled loose, gutter sagging or torn off</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standing water against fascia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rotted fascia board and soffit repairs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Blocked drainage at the eave</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Contributes to ice dams and water under shingles</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Downspout discharging at the wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Saturated soil, hydrostatic pressure, ice on paths</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The one worth dwelling on is the first. A modest roof sheds thousands of litres in a single storm, and a downspout delivering that volume to one point against the foundation is one of the most common reasons a basement that used to be dry stops being dry. Our guide to <a href="/wet-basement-solutions/">wet basement solutions</a> starts at the gutters for exactly this reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Ladder Safety First</h2>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Honest Warning</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Ladder falls are one of the leading causes of serious injury in home maintenance, and gutter cleaning is the job that puts most people on a ladder. On a two-storey house, on soft or sloping ground, or on a steep roof, hiring this out is a sensible decision rather than a failure of nerve.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are doing it yourself, set the ladder on firm, level ground at roughly a four-to-one angle — one unit out from the wall for every four units of height. Soft autumn ground is deceptive; a plank or a ladder levelling foot makes a real difference on grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use a stand-off, sometimes sold as a ladder stabiliser. It rests the ladder against the roof or wall rather than on the gutter itself, which both stops you crushing the gutter and gives a far more stable platform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Move the ladder rather than reaching. The single most common cause of a fall is stretching sideways to reach one more armful of leaves. Keep your belt buckle between the stiles, and accept that repositioning the ladder twenty times is part of the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Have someone else at home, footing the ladder or at least aware you are up there. And keep well clear of overhead electrical service lines where they enter the house — that is a hard stop, not a caution.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="method" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The Method</h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Start at the downspout and work outward.</strong> That way you are pushing debris away from the outlet rather than packing it in, and if a mass of material shifts it goes over the edge rather than into the pipe.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Scoop into a bucket.</strong> A plastic gutter scoop, or a gloved hand, into a bucket hung from a ladder hook. Dropping debris to the ground doubles the job and spreads wet leaf mulch across whatever is below. Wear gloves — gutters collect sharp metal edges, screws, and the occasional wasp nest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Flush with a hose.</strong> Once the bulk is out, run water from the far end toward the downspout. This does two things: it clears the fine grit and shingle granules that a scoop leaves behind, and it shows you how the gutter actually behaves. Water pooling anywhere means the fall is wrong; water appearing on the outside means a seam or a hole.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Check the fall.</strong> A gutter should drop roughly 5 mm for every 3 metres toward the outlet. Standing water after flushing means either a sagging section or a hanger that needs adjusting, and standing water is what freezes and pulls gutters off buildings.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A pressure washer is an option</strong>, ideally with a gutter cleaning attachment used from the ground, which is much safer than a ladder. Keep the pressure moderate — high pressure damages seams, lifts sealant and can drive water up under the shingles at the eave. Scoop the bulk first and use the washer for the flush. Our <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer testing</a> covers machines and attachments.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="downspouts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Downspouts and Discharge</h2>

          <p>
            A clean gutter feeding a blocked downspout is a clean gutter that still overflows. Test each one by running the hose into the top and watching the bottom — the flow should be immediate and full.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it is not, clear from the bottom upward. Feed the hose up from the outlet, or use a drain auger for a compacted blockage. Working downward tends to pack the blockage tighter, particularly where the pipe changes direction at an elbow, which is where blockages almost always sit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then look at where the water goes. This is the part people skip, and it matters as much as the cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Discharge should be at least a metre and a half from the foundation, onto ground that slopes away from the house. A downspout ending at the base of the wall is a machine for saturating the soil against your foundation, and in winter that saturated soil freezes and expands against it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep discharge off paths, steps and driveways. Meltwater running across a walkway on a February afternoon becomes an ice sheet by evening, and it is a genuinely dangerous one because it forms where people expect bare pavement.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a downspout feeds an underground drain, check that it flows freely before winter. A blocked underground line backs up, and once the standing water in it freezes there is nothing to be done until spring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The Inspection That Matters More</h2>

          <p>
            You are up a ladder with a clear view of the eaves once a year. Use it — the inspection is worth more than the scooping.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Hangers and brackets.</strong> Look for spikes backing out, loose screws, and sections sagging between supports. Ice is heavy, and a hanger that is marginal in November fails in January and takes a length of gutter with it. Tightening or adding a hanger takes minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Seams and end caps.</strong> Drips at a joint or an end cap mean failed sealant. Clean the area, dry it, and reseal with a gutter sealant while the weather still allows it to cure — most need above about 5°C.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The fascia behind the gutter.</strong> Push gently at the board. Soft or spongy timber means water has been sitting against it, probably for a while, and that is a repair to plan rather than ignore. Also check the soffit for staining, which shows where water has been overflowing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The roof edge.</strong> While you are there, look at the shingles at the eave for lifting, cracking or missing granules, and check any flashing where the roof meets a wall. These are the areas that ice dams attack first.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anything growing.</strong> Moss or seedlings in a gutter means it has held soil and water long enough to support plants, which tells you the fall or the cleaning schedule needs attention rather than just this year's clean.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ice-dams" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Ice Dams: The Real Cause</h2>

          <p>
            Ice dams get blamed on gutters, and gutters are mostly a bystander. The mechanism is heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Warm air escaping from the living space into the attic heats the roof deck. Snow on the upper roof melts, the meltwater runs down to the eave — which overhangs unheated space and stays cold — and refreezes there. That ridge of ice grows, backs water up behind it, and eventually that standing water works up under the shingles and into the roof structure and the ceilings below.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">What Actually Fixes It</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Air sealing and insulation in the attic, plus adequate ventilation to keep the roof deck cold and uniform.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Seal the air leaks first — recessed lights, the attic hatch, plumbing and wiring penetrations, the top plates of interior walls — then insulate to the depth your climate calls for, then confirm soffit and ridge ventilation is not blocked by insulation. Clean gutters help meltwater drain away, but they cannot prevent a dam that heat is creating.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If a dam has already formed, do not chip at it with anything sharp — you will damage the shingles you are trying to protect. Calcium chloride in a fabric sleeve laid across the dam melts a channel through it, and a roof rake used from the ground to pull snow off the lower roof reduces the material available to melt in the first place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="guards" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Are Gutter Guards Worth It?</h2>

          <p>
            They reduce the frequency of the job rather than eliminating it, and that is worth a lot on a two-storey house where every clean means a ladder or a contractor.
          </p>

          <p>
            What they do well is keep out leaves and larger debris. What they do less well is exclude fine material: shingle granules, seed pods, pollen and pine needles find their way in or accumulate on top of most designs, and the guards themselves need clearing periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Realistically, expect to inspect annually and clean every two or three years rather than every autumn. Note also that some designs make the eventual clean harder, because the guard has to come off first — a factor worth weighing at purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            In heavy snow country, check how a given design handles ice. Some solid-cover and reverse-curve guards can hold ice at the eave rather than shedding it. Our <a href="/best-gutter-guards/">gutter guard testing</a> covers how the main types perform, including in freezing conditions.
          </p>
        </div>


        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Heated Cables and What They Actually Do</h2>

          <p>
            Heated cables — zigzagged along the eave and often run down into the gutter and downspout — come up whenever ice dams do, and they are worth understanding before buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            What they do is keep a channel open so meltwater has a path off the roof rather than backing up behind a ridge of ice. What they do not do is stop the dam forming, because the heat leaking into your attic is still melting snow further up the roof. They are a symptom management tool, and a permanent running cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes them reasonable in two situations: a roof with a genuinely difficult geometry — a valley discharging onto a low eave, a north-facing dormer that never sees sun — and a house where the insulation work is planned but not yet done. As a substitute for fixing the attic, they are an expensive way to postpone the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you fit them, use a product with a thermostat or a self-regulating cable rather than one that runs whenever it is switched on, and route the cable into the downspout as well. A cable that melts a channel in the gutter but leaves the downspout frozen has simply moved the ice a metre.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaning the Ladder on the Gutter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It crushes the gutter and gives an unstable platform. Use a stand-off so the ladder bears on the roof or wall, and set the base at a four-to-one angle on firm level ground.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching Instead of Moving the Ladder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Stretching sideways for one more armful is how people fall. Keep your belt buckle between the stiles and accept that repositioning the ladder repeatedly is part of the job.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cleaning the Gutter but Not the Downspout</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A clean gutter feeding a blocked downspout still overflows. Test each one with a hose and clear blockages from the bottom upward, since working down packs them tighter at the elbow.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Where the Water Lands</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A downspout discharging at the foundation saturates the soil against it all winter, and one crossing a path builds an ice sheet. Extend discharge at least a metre and a half out, onto ground sloping away.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blaming Ice Dams on the Gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Dams are caused by attic heat melting snow that refreezes at the cold eave. Air sealing, insulation and ventilation fix them. Clean gutters help drainage but will not stop a dam heat is creating.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chipping at Ice With Something Sharp</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You will damage the shingles you are trying to protect. Use calcium chloride in a fabric sleeve to melt a channel, and a roof rake from the ground to reduce snow on the lower roof.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should you clean them?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">After the last leaves fall and before the first hard freeze — late autumn in most climates. Under heavy tree cover, plan two visits: one mid-autumn and one once the trees are bare.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What if you skip it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Water overflows at the foundation and causes basement damp, trapped water freezes and pulls hangers and fascia loose, and blocked drainage at the eaves contributes to ice dams pushing water under the shingles.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do guards end the job?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">No, they reduce its frequency. Fine grit, shingle granules, pine needles and seed pods still accumulate, and the guards need clearing. Expect annual inspection and a clean every two or three years.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes, ideally with a gutter attachment used from the ground, which is far safer than a ladder. Keep pressure moderate — it can damage seams and drive water under shingles — and scoop the bulk out first.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you stop ice dams?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Fix the attic. Heat escaping upward melts snow that refreezes at the cold eave. Air sealing, insulation and adequate ventilation are the real solutions; clean gutters help drainage but cannot prevent a dam.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Disconnect downspouts in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">No, but check the discharge. Route it at least a metre and a half from the wall onto ground sloping away, and keep it off paths and steps where meltwater will refreeze into an ice hazard.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often overall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Twice a year is the standard, but tree cover decides it — three or four times under heavy deciduous or pine cover, perhaps once with no overhanging trees. If you can see debris above the gutter line from the ground, it is overdue.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DIY safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">On a single storey with firm level ground and a good ladder, generally yes with sensible precautions. On two storeys, soft or sloping ground, or a steep roof, the risk changes considerably and hiring it out is a reasonable call.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

