        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Clean them after the leaves finish and before the first hard freeze</strong> — scooping from the downspout outward into a bucket, then flushing with a hose to clear the fines and reveal any pooling or leaks.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then do the two things that matter more than the scooping: confirm every downspout runs freely and discharges at least a metre and a half from the foundation on ground sloping away, and inspect the hangers, seams and fascia while you have a clear view of them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            And treat ice dams as an attic problem, because that is what they are. Air sealing, insulation and ventilation prevent them; clean gutters only give the meltwater somewhere to go. Above all, respect the ladder — on a two-storey house, hiring this out is the sensible answer.
          </p>
